Do not wait for the console ring for ever

Run 468 got the storage controller to see the device for the first time --
the panel says "nio: ufs bu queue-up dp=1 hcs=0000010f", and HCS.DP is the
bit that says whether the controller sees the device at all.  Every round
before it reported a clear DP.

The panel then stops.  Not with a panic and not with a stall: the beacon
keeps counting, because the beacon writes to the framebuffer directly,
while the thread that was printing does not come back.  The last thing
it printed was one line of progress.

There are two ways a thread stops right there, and this patch settles
one of them.  The first is the console ring: console_write() reserves
space in a ring, and when the ring is full it drains it and tries again,
and drains it with console_ring_try_empty(false).  Nothing else drains
that ring.  The thread that is printing is the only thread that can, so
if the drain does not empty it, the thread waits for a space that only
it can make, and it waits for ever.  The board's console is a video
console, and the drain can fail to empty the ring when another CPU is
inside it holding the flush lock.

So when a drain attempt has not given the space up, the characters are
written straight to the console instead.  The ring is only a buffer for
the hardware; writing to the hardware directly is what the ring does
anyway, and it is what console_write() already does when the console is
not initialized, when the machine is suspending, when the kernel
debugger is entered and when the code runs in the PPL.  Nothing is lost
and the thread keeps running.

The second way is the device: the thread was reading the controller's
status register when it stopped, and a read of a controller that has
been given work can hang.  That one is not settled here; the storage
driver prints around its reads so the next round says which it was.
diff --git a/osfmk/console/serial_console.c b/osfmk/console/serial_console.c
index e5b23f2..4c70372 100644
--- a/osfmk/console/serial_console.c
+++ b/osfmk/console/serial_console.c
@@ -558,6 +558,25 @@ console_write(char *str, int size)
 			}
 
 			console_ring_try_empty(false);
+
+			/*
+			 * nio: the ring did not give the space up after a drain
+			 * attempt.  Waiting for it here waits for ever: nothing
+			 * else drains this ring, so the thread that is printing
+			 * is the only one that can, and it is the one that is
+			 * waiting.  Write straight to the console instead.  The
+			 * ring is only a buffer for the hardware, and writing to
+			 * the hardware directly is what the ring does anyway --
+			 * it is what console_write() already does when the
+			 * console is not initialized, when the machine is
+			 * suspending, when the kernel debugger is entered and
+			 * when the code runs in the PPL.  Nothing is lost, and
+			 * the thread keeps running.
+			 */
+			if ((write_ptr = console_ring_reserve_space(chunk_size)) == NULL) {
+				_cnputs(str, size, true);
+				return;
+			}
 		}
 
 		for (i = 0; i < chunk_size; i++) {
